║  Unit 5.3: Lattice-Based Signatures and Rejection Sampling   ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

=== Information Leakage Attack Demo ===

Secret key s = (7, -4, 11)
(Attacker doesn't know this)

After 1000 signatures:
  498 with c=0, 502 with c=1

Average z when c=0: (1.54, -1.73, 0.20)
Average z when c=1: (6.56, -4.62, 9.27)

Recovered s = E[z|c=1] - E[z|c=0]:
  s ≈ (5.02, -2.89, 9.07)

Actual s = (7, -4, 11)

*** SECRET KEY RECOVERED! ***

=== Rejection Sampling Demo ===

Secret key s = (7, -4, 11)
Gaussian parameter σ = 14.0
Repetition rate M = 13.70 (target ~14 attempts/signature)

After 1000 signatures (avg 13.14 attempts each):
  Rejection rate: 92.4% of candidates rejected
  496 with c=0, 504 with c=1

Average z when c=0: (0.00, 0.73, -0.09)
Average z when c=1: (1.14, 1.06, 0.86)

Attempted s = E[z|c=1] - E[z|c=0]:
  s ≈ (1.14, 0.33, 0.95)

Actual s = (7, -4, 11)

*** With rejection sampling, attack fails! ***
(Both averages are near 0 - no difference to exploit)

=== SIS Problem Demo ===

Matrix A (2×4 over Z_101):
  [23 45 12 67]
  [78 34 89 11]

SIS Problem: Find short z ≠ 0 such that Az = 0 (mod 101)

Searching for solutions with ||z||_∞ ≤ 3...
  No solutions found with ||z||_∞ ≤ 3
  (This is expected - finding short solutions is hard!)

Note: With real parameters (n ≈ 256, q ≈ 2²³),
finding short solutions is computationally infeasible.

=== Fiat-Shamir with Aborts Demo ===

This demonstrates the signing process with aborts.

Attempting to sign with rejection sampling (M = 13.70):
  Attempt 1: c=1, accept_prob=0.037, random=0.524 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 2: c=0, accept_prob=0.073, random=0.231 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 3: c=1, accept_prob=0.026, random=0.222 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 4: c=1, accept_prob=0.090, random=0.230 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 5: c=0, accept_prob=0.073, random=0.360 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 6: c=0, accept_prob=0.073, random=0.661 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 7: c=0, accept_prob=0.073, random=0.231 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 8: c=1, accept_prob=0.041, random=0.787 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 9: c=0, accept_prob=0.073, random=0.369 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 10: c=1, accept_prob=0.028, random=0.637 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 11: c=1, accept_prob=0.066, random=0.826 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  Attempt 12: c=1, accept_prob=0.020, random=0.579 → REJECT (abort and retry)
  ... (further rejected attempts omitted) ...

Signature produced after 48 attempt(s)
z = (3, 4, -19)

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
Key Takeaways:
  1. Naive lattice signatures leak the secret key
  2. Rejection sampling makes output independent of secret
  3. Cost: Multiple attempts needed (Fiat-Shamir with Aborts)
  4. ML-DSA uses this framework with Module-LWE/SIS
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

==============================================
Overall result: PASS (signing demos produced valid signatures)
==============================================
